[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Tick API and DOM Flushes in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte is a compile-time framework that updates the DOM reactively whenever state changes. However, these updates do not happen synchronously. Instead, Svelte batches state changes and flushes them to the DOM asynchronously. This tutorial explores how to use the tick API to interact with the DOM after these updates are applied."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Svelte Schedules DOM Updates"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you modify a state variable in Svelte, the framework does not immediately update the DOM node. It waits until the next microtask to batch multiple state mutations together for performance optimization. Attempting to read a DOM property immediately after a state change will therefore yield stale results."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"let count = 0;
let div;

async function increment() {
  count += 1;
  // At this point, div.textContent is still outdated
  console.log(div.textContent); // Shows previous value
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Introducing the tick() Function"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The tick() function is a specialized utility imported from 'svelte'. It returns a promise that resolves as soon as any pending state changes have been applied to the DOM. By awaiting tick(), you can guarantee that your subsequent code runs against the latest DOM state."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import { tick } from 'svelte';

let count = 0;
let div;

async function increment() {
  count += 1;
  await tick();
  // Now the DOM has been updated
  console.log(div.textContent); // Shows current value
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Use Cases for tick()"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Auto-scrolling a chat container to the bottom when a new message is appended to the state."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Focusing an input element immediately after it is rendered conditionally via an {#if} block."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Measuring exact dimensions (offsetWidth, clientHeight) of elements that depend on dynamic text bindings."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Auto-Scrolling a Container"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import { tick } from 'svelte';

  let messages = ["Hello", "Welcome to Svelte"];
  let chatBox;

  async function addMessage() {
    messages = [...messages, "New message added!"];
    await tick();
    chatBox.scrollTop = chatBox.scrollHeight;
  }
</script>

<div bind:this={chatBox} style="height: 100px; overflow-y: auto;">
  {#each messages as msg}
    <p>{msg}</p>
  {/each}
</div>
<button on:click={addMessage}>Add Message</button>"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The tick API bridges the asynchronous gap between Svelte's reactive state management and imperative DOM measurements. By properly utilizing async/await with tick(), you can build robust components that reliably interact with newly rendered DOM nodes."}]
